From eb8e4dc9011fe80e2226faa6f396c71743689ae3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 3 <phase3@openwch.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 02:13:08 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] RISC-V: disassemble the WCH XW compressed encodings on
 request

objdump could assemble the XW compressed loads and stores but never showed
them again: the disassembler skipped INSN_CLASS_XW unconditionally and
printed .insn.  The official objdump prints them, but only when asked by
name, through a -M option the upstream disassembler does not have:

  $ objdump -d xw.o                 0: 2188   .insn   2, 0x2188
  $ objdump -d -Mxw xw.o            0: 2188   lbu     a0,0(a1)
  $ objdump -d -Mxw,no-aliases      0: 2188   c.lbu   a0,0(a1)

The option is the only thing that decides.  An object whose Tag_RISCV_arch
already contains xw2p2 still disassembles to .insn without it, and -Mmax
does not enable it either -- it decodes these halfwords as the standard
c.fld they collide with.  So the XW class is exempt from the architecture
test rather than driven by it, in the same shape the assembler already
uses for its own XW gates.

Which of several matching encodings wins stays a question of table order,
as for every other class, and the table needed correcting for it.  The XW
rows were appended after the encodings they overlap, which is invisible
while nothing decodes them and wrong as soon as something does: an
exhaustive sweep of all 65,536 halfwords against the official objdump puts
the damage at 8,192 words on an rv32imafdc_xw object, where
-Mxw,no-aliases reached c.fld before the XW form, and 512 more on an
rv32imac_zcb object, where it reached a Zcb load.  Move the four XW stack
forms and the four mnemonics XW shares with Zcb ahead of the Zcd and Zcb
rows, under three constraints: each mnemonic's rows stay contiguous, as the
table requires; the XW row of a shared mnemonic stays first, which is the
ordering the assembler's own XW gates rely on; and the Zcb rows that move
along (0x8000-0x8FFF, op=00) stay disjoint from the Zcd ranges they pass
(c.fld 0x2000-0x3FFF, c.fsd 0xA000-0xBFFF), so no new Zcb-versus-Zcd
precedence is introduced.  The XW alias rows are already
interleaved by mnemonic and are left alone -- they were reproducing the
official choice exactly, including the cases where a Zcb alias
legitimately wins.

Register the option, exempt the class from the architecture test while
gating it on the option, and add the operand printers for the four XW
address modifiers, which existed only on the assembler side because
nothing had ever printed one.  The help text is the official wording.

Behavior source: direct WCH 15.2.0 objdump probes on an object holding all
eight encodings, across -Mxw, -Mmax, -Mxw,max, -Mxw,no-aliases and an XW
arch attribute with no option; the 65,536-halfword sweep over four
architecture profiles and four disassembler modes, which agrees with the
official objdump in all sixteen cells after the move and leaves the eight
pre-existing cells unchanged; and the 8,704-case XW producer matrix, whose
encodings the move leaves byte-identical.  The same option-not-attribute
policy on the GDB side of the shared opcodes library is recorded in
  analysis/toolchain/phase3e-gdb-sim-deep-diff.md

The rows this moves carry no xlen requirement, so the move applies to
elf64 objects on the same terms: -Mxw decodes the XW halfwords there too,
which is what the official objdump does, measured in
  tmp/phase9-evidence/15.2.0/spec.md
